---
/**
 * Homepage attorneys band — the three partners, as a static row of three cards.
 *
 * ⚠️ THIS IS A REWORK, NOT THE ARTBOARD. The ATTORNEYS band of
 * `Cohen & Jaffe Homepage v1.dc.html` (lines 511–579) was built as drawn, shown
 * to the client and REJECTED on 2026-09-08 — the design, not the build. What is
 * here now is what was asked for in its place, and each change undoes something
 * the board does, so the board is no longer a useful reference for this section.
 * Do not "restore" any of it from the artboard:
 *
 *   - NO CAROUSEL. It had one briefly, four across over all six attorneys, and
 *     it is gone with its track, its arrows and its script.
 *   - THREE PARTNERS ONLY — Cohen, Jaffe and Tiger. Which three is a decision
 *     of the SECTION — `homePage.attorneys.attorneys[]` — not a flag on the
 *     documents. There is no `featured` field on `attorney` and there must not
 *     be one; all six still belong on `/about/attorneys/`.
 *   - NO SIGNATURE. The board sets the attorney's name in Mrs Saint Delafield
 *     under the quote; it is cut. `.signature` in global.css now has no consumer.
 *     (The QUOTE itself was cut with it and later restored — see below.)
 *   - NO CARD BLURB, and no field behind it. `attorney.summary` ("Card blurb")
 *     was `.required()`, fed this one paragraph and nothing else on the site,
 *     and was removed from the schema and unset on all six documents.
 *   - THE CARD IS A PANEL. White on the sand strip with a hairline border,
 *     which the board's borderless card is not.
 *   - THE ROLE AND THE BIO LINK SHARE ONE LINE, held apart. The board stacks a
 *     "Full profile →" link under a paragraph of blurb. ⚠️ The link was a
 *     full-width ghost BUTTON for one iteration and was cut back as
 *     "overwhelming": on a card holding nothing but a portrait, a name and a
 *     role, a 48px bordered control outweighed everything above it.
 *   - THE CLOSING BANNER IS ONE CENTRED BUTTON, with no rule over it. The board
 *     holds "Meet the full team" against three circular staff portraits and a
 *     line naming the three attorneys the row leaves out, ruled off with a
 *     hairline. Portraits, then line, then hairline — which by then was fencing
 *     off a single centred control.
 *     ⚠️ It is the section's only route to `/about/attorneys/`.
 *   - THE HEADING DROPS THE BOARD'S "three". It has been both ways twice; this
 *     is a choice, not arithmetic.
 *
 * What survives the rework: a square portrait, the name, the role, a link to the
 * bio, and a closing call to action.
 *
 * THE QUOTES ON THESE CARDS ARE THE ARTBOARD'S WORDING, AND THE FIRM HAS
 * CONFIRMED THEM AS REAL (2026-09-08). That confirmation matters and is why it
 * is written down: `scripts/seed-attorneys.ts` seeded `attorney.quote` from the
 * live site instead, on the reasoning that the board's versions were unsourced,
 * and all three were replaced in the Studio. The firm has since said these are
 * the quotes they are using. **`seed-attorneys.ts` is now stale on this field —
 * see the warning in its own docblock; do not re-run it.**
 *
 * ⚠️ JAFFE'S CARD AND THE "OUR GOALS" BAND CARRY THE SAME SENTENCE. That band
 * prints "I worked ambulances before I practiced law, so I know what a serious
 * injury does to a family. We handle these cases personally."; his card prints
 * the same line without its last clause. Both are approved copy, so this is a
 * repetition on one page rather than an accuracy problem — but it IS the thing
 * `attorneyQuote.text` and `attorney.quote` are separate fields to prevent, and
 * nothing in the model can stop the same words being typed into both. Raised
 * 2026-09-08 and still open; one of the two bands should give it up.
 *
 * THE PLAY BUTTON IS CONDITIONAL. `wistiaId` is optional on `attorney`, and the
 * badge renders only for someone who has one — Tiger has none, which is what
 * the empty state looks like.
 *
 * ⚠️ COHEN'S AND JAFFE'S VIDEOS ARE PLACEHOLDERS. Both point at `c6b0eghb5r`,
 * the firm's single Wistia upload and a general attorney explainer, added so the
 * button and the lightbox could be seen and clicked. **Neither is their film.**
 * The same clip is already standing in on the first case-result card, the "Our
 * goals" video card and all four video reviews. Unset both before launch or
 * replace them: a play button over a named attorney's portrait is a promise that
 * the video is of that attorney, which is a stronger claim than the other four
 * placeholders make. See `scripts/patch-attorney-portraits.ts`.
 */
import { urlFor } from "../lib/image";
import type { HOME_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT } from "../sanity/sanity.types";

type Section = NonNullable<NonNullable<HOME_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT>["attorneys"]>;

interface Props {
  section: Section;
}

const { section } = Astro.props;
const attorneys = section.attorneys ?? [];

/**
 * The widths the square portrait is served at. At three across the card reaches
 * ~470px on the widest board, so 800 covers a 2x display comfortably.
 *
 * NOT `srcSet()` from `lib/image`, which only sets a width. A width alone
 * leaves the CDN serving the source's own 9:16 portrait ratio, and CSS then
 * crops two thirds of every byte away with `object-fit`. Asking for
 * `.height(w)` as well makes the CDN do the square crop — around the HOTSPOT,
 * which is why `attorney.portrait` asks for one — so what arrives is what is
 * drawn.
 */
const CARD_WIDTHS = [480, 640, 800];

const squareSrcSet = (portrait: Parameters<typeof urlFor>[0]) =>
  CARD_WIDTHS.map((w) => `${urlFor(portrait).width(w).height(w).url()} ${w}w`).join(", ");
---

<section class="attorneys section" aria-labelledby="attorneys-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="attorneys__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">{section.eyebrow}</p>
      <h2 class="attorneys__heading" id="attorneys-heading">{section.heading}</h2>
    </header>

    {
      attorneys.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="attorneys__grid" role="list">
          {attorneys.map((attorney) => (
            <li class="atty">
              <div class="atty__media">
                <img
                  class="atty__image"
                  src={urlFor(attorney.portrait).width(800).height(800).url()}
                  srcset={squareSrcSet(attorney.portrait)}
                  sizes="(max-width: 700px) 92vw, (max-width: 1024px) 46vw, 31vw"
                  alt={attorney.portrait.alt ?? ""}
                  width="800"
                  height="800"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />

                {attorney.wistiaId && (
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="atty__play"
                    data-video-id={attorney.wistiaId}
                    aria-label={`Play video: ${attorney.name}`}
                  >
                    <span class="atty__play-badge" aria-hidden="true">
                      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="currentColor">
                        <path d="M7 5v14l10-7z" />
                      </svg>
                    </span>
                  </button>
                )}
              </div>

              <div class="atty__body">
                <h3 class="atty__name">{attorney.name}</h3>

                {attorney.quote && (
                  <blockquote class="atty__quote">
                    <p>&ldquo;{attorney.quote}&rdquo;</p>
                  </blockquote>
                )}

                <div class="atty__meta">
                  <p class="atty__role">{attorney.role}</p>
                  <a class="atty__link link-arrow" href={`/about/attorneys/${attorney.slug}/`}>
                    View Profile
                    <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                      &rarr;
                    </span>
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }

    {
      section.cta && (
        <div class="attorneys__foot">
          <a class="btn btn--green btn--sm btn--wide" href={section.cta.href}>
            {section.cta.label}
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">
              &rarr;
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .attorneys {
    background-color: var(--sand);
  }

  /* --- Header: centred over the row --------------------------------------- */

  .attorneys__head {
    text-align: center;
    margin-block-end: var(--space-44);
  }

  .attorneys__heading {
    font-size: var(--fs-section);
    /* The board sets this `white-space: nowrap` on a fixed 1660px board, where
       the line happens to fit. It must wrap here — at 375px it is five lines. */
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  /* --- The row -------------------------------------------------------------
     A plain grid. There is no scroller and no script: with the band fixed at
     three partners there is nothing a carousel could reveal, and a row of three
     is the one case that fits at every width down to the phone breakpoint.

     `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than `1fr`: a grid track's default `min-width: auto`
     resolves to min-content, which an unbreakable name would push the column
     past.

     The gap is wider than the reviews band's `--space-20` because these cards
     are BORDERED. Two hairlines 20px apart read as one banded strip rather than
     three cards; at 32 they separate. The board's own 80px was drawn for
     borderless cards floating on the sand and is far too much for panels. */

  .attorneys__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-32);
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* The card IS a panel, on the client's instruction — white with a hairline,
     which the board's card is not. `overflow: hidden` is what makes the
     portrait's top corners follow the card's radius; the portrait carries no
     radius of its own. */
  .atty {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* --- Portrait ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .atty__media {
    position: relative;
    /* The board's square crop, and the reason `attorney.portrait` asks for a
       hotspot on the face — every other use of the same photograph is taller. */
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    /* `flex: none` so the portrait keeps its square while the body below
       stretches to equalise card heights across the row. */
    flex: none;
    background-color: var(--border-strong);
  }

  /* `object-fit` is a SAFETY NET here, not the framing. The CDN is asked for a
     square (`.width(w).height(w)`), and it crops that square around the
     portrait's HOTSPOT — which is why `attorney.portrait` asks for one and says
     to put it on the face. So the delivered image already matches this box and
     `cover` has nothing to crop; it only earns its keep if a portrait is ever
     uploaded at a ratio the CDN cannot satisfy. The board's `object-position:
     50% 6%` is deliberately NOT carried over: it was correcting a centre crop
     of a 9:16 source, and against a hotspot crop it would fight the hotspot. */
  .atty__image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* The whole frame is the hit area, not the badge alone — same as the reviews
     band. Transparent, and it needs an explicit z-index because it would
     otherwise paint under the image it follows. */
  .atty__play {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .atty__play:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--gold-ink);
    outline-offset: -4px;
  }

  /* Bottom-right and white, which is the board's own badge — NOT the reviews
     band's centred gold one. A portrait is a face, and the reviews badge sits
     dead centre; over a person that is exactly where it must not go. */
  .atty__play-badge {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-end: var(--space-20);
    inset-block-end: var(--space-20);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    padding-inline-start: 3px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--white);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    color: var(--forest-700);
    transition:
      background-color var(--duration) var(--ease),
      scale var(--duration) var(--ease);
  }

  .atty__play:hover .atty__play-badge,
  .atty__play:focus-visible .atty__play-badge {
    background-color: var(--cream);
    scale: 1.06;
  }

  /* --- Card body ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  .atty__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Stretches so the link can pin to the foot of the tallest card. */
    flex: 1;
    padding: var(--space-28);
  }

  .atty__name {
    font-size: var(--fs-subsection);
    line-height: var(--lh-subhead);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-16);
  }

  /* ⚠️ 19px, NOT the board's 21px, and the card it was measured against no
     longer exists. The board sets the quote at 21 over a 32px name, a script
     signature and a blurb — four things sharing the card, so 21 was already the
     junior voice. Everything but the name is gone, which would have left 21px
     italic reading as a second heading directly under a 32px one. `--fs-19` is
     the site's lead step and puts a clear rung between the two.

     `--lh-lead` (1.5) rather than the board's 1.45: this is the only running
     copy on the card and it runs three to four lines, where the board's had a
     signature and a blurb under it to break the block up.

     Ink rather than `--body`, which is the board's own choice — the quote is
     the person speaking and should carry, even set small. */
  .atty__quote {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--fs-19);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: var(--lh-lead);
    color: var(--color-heading);
    /* The FLOOR of the gap above the meta row. `.atty__meta` pins itself with
       `margin-block-start: auto`, and auto collapses to ZERO once the copy
       fills the column — so on the longest quote the last line would sit on the
       role. Flex margins do not collapse, so this and the auto ADD UP: 24px is
       the floor, free space is whatever is left over. Same pattern as
       `.review__prose` in the reviews band. */
    margin-block-end: var(--space-24);
  }

  .atty__role {
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* `--lh-label`, never `--lh-flat`: tracked uppercase micro-type wraps on a
       narrow card, and two flat-leaded lines collide. Three of the six roles in
       the collection are long enough to take two lines. */
    line-height: var(--lh-label);
    color: var(--gold-ink);
  }

  /* The role and the bio link share one line, held apart.

     ⚠️ `flex-wrap` IS LOAD-BEARING, not defensive tidiness. The two together
     want ~235px and the card is 165px of usable width at the 768 breakpoint, so
     below roughly 900px the link WILL drop to its own line — and it lands at the
     start of that line, because `space-between` on a single item resolves to
     flex-start. Without the wrap the two would collide instead.

     THE ROW IS PINNED TO THE FOOT, and it only became right to pin it when the
     quote came back. While the role sat directly under the name it BELONGED to
     it, and pinning stranded it mid-card the moment one name wrapped to two
     lines and its neighbours did not. With a quote between them this row is a
     FOOTER — title and action, closing the card — so pinning is what keeps every
     link in the row on one line while three quotes of different lengths run
     above them. */
  .atty__meta {
    margin-block-start: auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-8) var(--space-16);
  }

  /* --- Closing banner ------------------------------------------------------
     One centred button, no rule over it, and the section's ONLY route to
     `/about/attorneys/`.

     The artboard's version of this row is three circular staff portraits and the
     line "Six attorneys and a support staff of more than twenty…" held against
     the button. The portraits went first, then the line, both on the client's
     instruction — so what is left is centred rather than pushed to one end,
     because a lone control held right against nothing reads as a layout that
     lost something.

     The button is `--green`, not the cards' ghost, and the two are a hierarchy
     rather than an inconsistency: this is the band's one primary action and the
     board sets it solid green at exactly this size. Three ghost buttons above a
     fourth solid one reads as three peers and a conclusion.

     THE HAIRLINE IS GONE TOO, on the client's instruction. The board rules this
     row off, but that rule was drawn to separate the button from the paragraph
     and portraits beside it — with those gone it was fencing off a single
     centred control, which reads as heavier furniture than the control deserves.
     Space does the same work. The gap is therefore a plain margin rather than
     the margin-plus-padding the border used to sit between. */

  .attorneys__foot {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-60);
  }

  /* ---- Responsive ---------------------------------------------------------
     Not from the designs — the artboard is a fixed 1660px board with no mobile
     counterpart. These blocks MUST stay after the rules they override; a media
     query carries no extra specificity, so source order is what decides. */

  /* ⚠️ THERE IS NO TWO-UP STEP, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. There was one at 1024,
     and with exactly THREE cards it stranded the third alone on a second row
     beside a card's worth of empty space — a worse result than the narrow
     columns it was avoiding. Three items only ever divide by three or by one.

     What makes three-up hold down to the phone is that the rework emptied the
     card: it is a portrait, a name and a role, so at ~220px it is still
     comfortable, where the earlier version's 21px serif quote was not. If a
     quote or a blurb ever comes back, this breakpoint has to be re-measured —
     and the answer will be one-up sooner, never two-up. */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .attorneys__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-24);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .atty__play:hover .atty__play-badge,
    .atty__play:focus-visible .atty__play-badge {
      scale: none;
    }
  }
</style>
